WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.660
How do you find which compartment holds a protein?

2
00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:08.640
Chapter 1 showed how pumps and leaks help compartments keep distinct chemistries.

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.020
To relate that chemistry to a protein, first find where the protein sits.

4
00:00:17.533 --> 00:00:21.173
A fluorescence image can show a protein at the lysosome.

5
00:00:22.173 --> 00:00:23.433
But at is not inside.

6
00:00:24.753 --> 00:00:28.393
That image alone cannot tell which side of the membrane it is on.

7
00:00:29.417 --> 00:00:31.537
So labs also take cells apart.

8
00:00:32.817 --> 00:00:37.117
They break cells and separate the pieces by a series of centrifuge spins.

9
00:00:38.697 --> 00:00:43.217
Organelles overlap across these crude fractions; no fraction is assumed pure.

10
00:00:48.217 --> 00:00:53.057
To read the fractions, measure markers, proteins whose home is already known.

11
00:00:54.417 --> 00:00:56.197
Histone H3 marks the nucleus.

12
00:00:57.697 --> 00:00:59.597
Cathepsin D marks the lysosome.

13
00:01:00.897 --> 00:01:02.797
Pyruvate kinase marks the cytosol.

14
00:01:04.097 --> 00:01:07.137
Each marker spreads across the fractions in its own shape.

15
00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:12.570
A protein that stays associated tends to travel with its compartment.

16
00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:18.590
So an unknown is assigned provisionally to the marker group whose full profile it

17
00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:19.590
most closely shares.

18
00:01:20.783 --> 00:01:25.323
In 2016, Georg Borner's group did this with mass spectrometry.

19
00:01:26.543 --> 00:01:31.943
They reported localization information for more than 8,700 HeLa proteins.

20
00:01:33.483 --> 00:01:35.943
Curated markers guided organellar assignments.

21
00:01:37.323 --> 00:01:41.743
Proteins split between compartments can yield mixed profiles and uncertain

22
00:01:41.743 --> 00:01:42.743
assignments.

23
00:01:43.333 --> 00:01:44.873
So a match is provisional.

24
00:01:46.173 --> 00:01:51.053
You would want a second marker that agrees, repeated fractionations, and an imaging

25
00:01:51.053 --> 00:01:52.513
check in intact cells.

26
00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:57.667
One more test asks whether an enzyme is enclosed by a membrane.

27
00:01:59.047 --> 00:02:05.147
In a 1951 report, de Duve's group found acid phosphatase acting less than expected

28
00:02:05.147 --> 00:02:06.207
in fresh fractions.

29
00:02:07.647 --> 00:02:10.647
More activity appeared once the particles were damaged.

30
00:02:11.717 --> 00:02:17.197
Picture the enzyme sealed in the lumen, its substrate outside and unable to cross.

31
00:02:18.717 --> 00:02:20.697
Now add detergent to open the membranes.

32
00:02:22.037 --> 00:02:23.417
What happens to the activity?

33
00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:33.820
It rises, as long as detergent does not change the enzyme itself.

34
00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.740
The substrate can now reach it.

35
00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:40.820
Activity hidden behind a membrane is called latency.

36
00:02:45.533 --> 00:02:50.893
Following these hidden enzymes across fractions led his group to propose the

37
00:02:50.893 --> 00:02:59.053
lysosome in 1955. De Duve later shared the 1974 Nobel Prize in Physiology or

38
00:02:59.053 --> 00:03:00.053
Medicine.

39
00:03:00.517 --> 00:03:05.297
The wall that holds a lumen's chemistry also hides what sits inside it.

40
00:03:06.317 --> 00:03:10.397
So place the protein first, by its whole profile and independent checks.

41
00:03:11.657 --> 00:03:14.517
Then ask what its compartment's chemistry does for it.
